To the Aithos team,

I am applying for the Research Engineer role because it is the closest match =
to what I actually build. I work on AI safety from the build-and-test side: I=
 design and run evaluations that check whether an AI system does what it shou=
ld under the conditions a real user or a regulator would test, and I build th=
e code so those checks are reproducible, public, and rerunnable by anyone who=
 wants to question them.

The most direct proof is a five-test red-team harness I published (github.com=
/Teamthy/ai-redteam-five-tests). It runs prompt injection, retrieval poisonin=
g, tool description poisoning, and multilingual refusal tests against any Ope=
nAI-compatible model, resolves tool calls, records the raw responses, and rep=
orts each failure with its sample size and rate. It is written to the standar=
d the role asks for: clean, tested, documented Python that others can build o=
n, not a one-off script. Every result traces back to a specific prompt, model=
, and commit, which is the part I care about most. I would not report a numbe=
r I could not reproduce.

That reproducibility mindset is why Aithos stands out to me. Turning every pi=
ece of research into a benchmark, open tooling, or reproducible methodology, =
and making work that regulators and other teams can actually use, is the bar =
I already hold my own work to. It is also what your principles describe: the =
values a model encodes should stay open and contestable rather than converge =
on one ideal, and a result is only as strong as the method that produced it. =
So I publish the tests and not just the findings, which is the difference bet=
ween a claim someone has to trust and one they can rerun.

My public series, Breaking AI, applies the same discipline to the question I =
started from: shipping AI that survives real users. The failures I write abou=
t are the boring ones (the prompt is not a wall, retrieved documents are untr=
usted input, tool descriptions are trusted unread, test sets that only cover =
one language), and the controls I recommend are engineering controls, not slo=
gans.

A few things I want to be straight about. I am a self-taught practitioner, no=
t a formal ML researcher, so I have no peer-reviewed papers. I have published=
 a public, reproducible body of work instead, and I build with the discipline=
 the role describes: safe sandboxes for code-running agents, careful handling=
 of credentials, and a habit of checking AI coding output rather than trustin=
g it. I have done tool-calling and agentic scaffolding but less formal multi-=
agent simulation, and I have not used Inspect yet; both are quick for me to p=
ick up given the evaluation work I already do. I am based in Lagos (UTC+1), s=
o my hours line up with Amsterdam, and I can work remotely; I am open to disc=
ussing the in-person days.

I would welcome the screening call, and honestly the three-hour take-home on =
a real Aithos evaluation is the part I would most like to do.

My resume is attached, and the harness repository is public if you want to re=
ad the code before we speak.

Olusanya Timothy
olusanyatimothy54@gmail.com =C2=B7 +234 812 786 8211 =C2=B7 linkedin.com/in/o=
lusanya-timothy =C2=B7 github.com/Teamthy

